  ],
  "details": "<p>The Roman villa at Varignano Vecchio was built in the early first century B.C., on the site of a residential building that marks the first settlement a few decades earlier. The main architectural phases date to:</p>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>the\u00a0period of Sulla, when the complex was built in its entirety with a large and monumental <em>pars urbana</em> and a <em>pars fructuaria</em>, from which the <em>torcularia</em> are archaeologically preserved;</li>\r\n<li>the first half of the first century A.D., when new thermal baths were built together with an imposing water cistern, while oil production was discontinued;</li>\r\n<li>the late fourth century A.D., when large transformations occur in the plan of the complex, but more detailed traces from this phase are not otherwise recognisable due to ploughing.</li>\r\n</ol>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "A villa maritima in eastern Liguria, inhabited from ca. 100 BC to the fifth century AD.",
